      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded in 1800 by Swiss Mennonite Joseph Johns, Johnstown was first called Conemaugh before adopting its current name in 1834.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Johnstown-Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Conemaugh River originates in Johnstown where the Stonycreek and Little Conemaugh rivers merge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnstown%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1889 flood buried Johnstown in mud and debris, a tragedy that has shaped the city’s identity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        2
